style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5005076"} "Jacques de Saint-Pastou"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]]] [:tr {:class "hidden_referees", :style "display: none"} [:th {} "Judge"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5005077"} "Georges Salles"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Twenty-one (21) architects, including one woman, from seven countries took part, exhibiting a total of 17 projects. Initially, the jury did not want to award medals, but reversed this decision on the intervention of " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Pierre de Coubertin"] ", but stated that no work was really worthy of an award."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Former Hungarian swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/48186"} "Alfréd Hajós"] ", who won two gold medals at the first Olympic Games 1896 in Athens, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/222"} "Dezső Lauber"] ", former Hungarian tennis player and participant in the 1908 London Games, produced a " [:em {} "Plan for a Stadium"] ", for which they received the silver medal. Lauber was an architect by trade and later became a secretary-general of the Hungarian Olympic Committee. Hajós was one of only two Olympians, the other being " [:a {:href "/athletes/44792"} "Walter Winans"] ", to win medals in both sport and art competitions."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The gold medal was not awarded. Bronze went to " [:a {:href "/athletes/921042"} "Julien Médécin"] " from Monaco. This was the first and, as of 2024, only medal Monaco has won at the Olympic Games. The architect received the medal for his work " [:em {} "Stadium for Monte Carlo"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Catalogue Number"